Pure Business Logic Unit Tests
TaskFlow - Full Stack Developer 2026

Extract deterministic rules first
The easiest test is often enabled by the best code shape. A pure policy function accepts values and
returns/throws based only on those values. No Nest module, no database, no HTTP, no global clock.

Status transition example
export function assertTaskStatusTransition(
from: TaskStatus,
to: TaskStatus,
) {
const allowed = ALLOWED_TRANSITIONS[from]
if (!allowed.includes(to)) {
throw new TaskStatusTransitionNotAllowed(from, to)
}
}

describe("assertTaskStatusTransition", () => {
it.each([
["TODO", "IN_PROGRESS"],
["TODO", "CANCELED"],
["IN_PROGRESS", "DONE"],
["DONE", "IN_PROGRESS"],
])("allows %s -> %s", (from, to) => {
expect(() => assertTaskStatusTransition(from, to)).not.toThrow()
})

it("rejects DONE -> TODO", () => {
expect(() => assertTaskStatusTransition("DONE", "TODO"))
.toThrow(TaskStatusTransitionNotAllowed)
})
})

Time should be injectable
• Avoid new Date() buried inside a rule if the rule needs deterministic testing.

• Pass now into the policy or inject a Clock abstraction only when multiple services genuinely need it.

• Use fake timers for code that is actually timer-driven, not as a default for ordinary date comparisons.

